.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Superposing a uniform stream of speed U_\infty with a doublet of strength \kappa = 2\pi U_\infty R^2 places a circular streamline of radius R exactly at the origin -- a solid cylinder, in potential-flow terms. Adding a point vortex of circulation \Gamma at the same location keeps that circle intact (a vortex centered on the circle induces purely tangential velocity there) while breaking its front-back symmetry, generating lift with no viscosity anywhere in the calculation. This example builds that flow with flow_past_cylinder, plots its surface pressure coefficient">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/fluids/potential_flow/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_cylinder_with_lift_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/fluids/potential_flow/plot_cylinder_with_lift`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Flow past a lifting cylinder</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/fluids/potential_flow/plot_cylinder_with_lift`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Jean le Rond d&#x27;Alembert applied the equations of inviscid, irrotational flow to steady motion past a closed body and found -- to his own evident discomfort -- that the theory predicts exactly zero net drag on the body no matter its shape, a flatly unphysical result for anything actually moving through a real fluid. flow_past_cylinder with its default circulation=0.0 builds exactly this symmetric, inviscid flow: a uniform stream past a circular cylinder with no circulation added, and therefore no front-back (or top-bottom) asymmetry anywhere in the pressure field. Integrating the resulting surface pressure coefficient">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/fluids/potential_flow/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_dalembert_paradox_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/fluids/potential_flow/plot_dalembert_paradox`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">D'Alembert's paradox: zero net drag on a cylinder in inviscid flow</div>
    </div>


